    <DataNode elementId="AT3G55120" textLabel="AT3G55120" type="GeneProduct">
      <Xref identifier="AT3G55120" dataSource="TAIR gene name" />
      <Graphics centerX="340" centerY="110" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment>chalcone isomerase. This is probably the locus described in the literature as tt5. (check on this) The results suggested, that tt5 corresponds to the chalcone isomerase (CHI) gene. Analysis of gene expression showed that CHI mRNA levels were similar to wild-type levels in the DFR (dihydroflavonol-4-reductase) null mutant tt3, which would be expected to accumulate flavonols. Similarly, wild-type levels of DFR mRNA were found in the apparent absence of CHI activity and consequently the absence of DFR substrates in tt5. This suggests that CHI and DFR gene expression may not be affected by changes in the concentrations of flavonoid biosynthetic intermediates. |CITS: [1354004]|</Comment>
